    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d1108">

                <pb facs="1063" n="1051" sameAs="frus:frus1943v04/pg1051"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">890E.00/313: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Diplomatic Agent and Consul General at Beirut</hi>
                        (<hi rendition="#italic">Wadsworth</hi>)
                        <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Beirut</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1943-12-16T12:00:00+02:00">December 16,
                            1943—noon.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received 4:15 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>347. On November 6 Lebanese Foreign Minister handed me letter<anchor n="60"
                        xml:id="d1108-f1"/> addressed to President Roosevelt by Sheikh Beshara El
                    Khouri announcing his assumption on September 21 of Lebanese Presidency. It
                    varied in minor detail only from similar letter<anchor n="61" xml:id="d1108-f2"
                    /> written by Syrian President enclosed with my despatch 190, October 2.<anchor
                        n="62" xml:id="d1108-f3"/>
                </p>
                <p>Five days after the receipt of Lebanese letter President Khouri was jailed. At
                    the end of Lebanese crisis I was summoned to Cairo. <pb facs="1064" n="1052"
                        xml:id="pg1052"/> There I delivered letter personally to President
                        Roosevelt<anchor n="63" xml:id="d1108-f4"/> who signed reply<anchor n="64"
                        xml:id="d1108-f5"/> prepared at his request by George Allen<anchor n="65"
                        xml:id="d1108-f6"/> and myself.</p>
                <p>This reply is conventional except for short passage reading “events of recent
                    weeks in your country have been followed in mine with very special attention and
                    sympathy”. It was delivered to Lebanese President through Foreign Minister
                    December 13.</p>
                <p>Yesterday Foreign Minister asked me to ascertain by telegraph whether my
                    Government would kindly agree to immediate publication here of this exchange of
                    Presidential letters. Full copies will be forwarded to Department by early
                    pouch.</p>
                <p>If such publication is approved, local outpost OWI wishes give matter effective
                    publicity and enquires whether Department cannot at the same time authorize
                    publication of exchange of letters with Syrian President, provided he agree
                        thereto.<anchor n="66" xml:id="d1108-f7"/>
                </p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Wadsworth</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d1108-f1" n="60" xml:id="d1108-f1-content">
                <p>Copy transmitted to the Department by the Diplomatic Agent in his despatch No.
                    241, December 22, 1943, not printed.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d1108-f2" n="61" xml:id="d1108-f2-content">
                <p>Dated August 17, <ref target="#pg985">p. 985</ref>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d1108-f3" n="62" xml:id="d1108-f3-content">
                <p>Not printed.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d1108-f4" n="63" xml:id="d1108-f4-content">
                <p>In Cairo for the First Cairo Conference, November 22–26, 1943; for correspondence
                    on this Conference, see <ref target="frus1943CairoTehran">
                        <hi rendition="#italic">Foreign Relations</hi>, The Conferences at Cairo and
                        Tehran, 1943</ref>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d1108-f5" n="64" xml:id="d1108-f5-content">
                <p>
                    <ref target="frus1943CairoTehran#pg_778">
                        <hi rendition="#italic">Ibid.</hi>, p. 778</ref>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d1108-f6" n="65" xml:id="d1108-f6-content">
                <p>Assistant Chief of the Division of Near Eastern Affairs.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d1108-f7" n="66" xml:id="d1108-f7-content">
                <p>In Department’s telegram No. 301 of December 22, to Beirut, it was stated that
                    “Department has no objection to publication of exchanges of letters … provided
                    you are satisfied that such publication at this time will not be regarded by the
                    French as provocative or as prejudicial to the working out of an amicable
                    agreement.” (890E.00/337a)</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
